    because `display: block` here would otherwise beat the UA rule.
  */
  .feed-hero-embed-note {
    margin: 0.55rem 0 0;
    max-width: 38ch;
    display: block;
    color: var(--color-white-muted, #888888);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .feed-hero-embed-note[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }

  /*
    The hub CTA. Same metrics as the SUBSCRIBE button beside it so the two read
    as one control group; the only differences are the glyph sitting AFTER the
    label (it is a "go there" arrow, not an icon for the thing) and a hover that
    picks up the resolved hub's own colour through --brand-color.
  */
  .feed-hero-hub-btn {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.45rem;
    margin-top: 0.15rem;
    padding: 0.5rem 0.9rem 0.5rem 1.05rem;
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.28);
    border-radius: 0;
    background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.32);
    color: var(--color-white, #f0f0f0);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: 0.62rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    text-decoration: none;
    cursor: pointer;
    transition: border-color 0.25s ease, background 0.25s ease, box-shadow 0.25s ease;
  }

  .feed-hero-hub-btn:hover,
  .feed-hero-hub-btn:focus-visible {
    border-color: rgba(var(--brand-rgb, 204, 0, 0), 0.85);
    background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5);
    box-shadow: 0 0 18px rgba(var(--brand-rgb, 204, 0, 0), 0.28);
  }

  /* The readable red, per scripts/a11y-tokens.test.mjs: this ring sits on dark
     artwork and #cc0000 is only 3.21:1. */
  .feed-hero-hub-btn:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--color-accent-text, #ef4444);
    outline-offset: 3px;
  }

  .feed-hero-hub-glyph {
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .feed-hero-hub-btn {
      transition: none;
    }
  }

  /*
    ─── SUBSCRIBE AND WATCH ON YOUTUBE ARE ONE DESIGN ────────────────────────

    Shared by SELECTOR, not by a second copy of the declarations. The escape
    link shipped first as quiet underlined text on the reasoning that it was a
    fallback rather than a third call to action, and next to a bordered
    SUBSCRIBE that read as unstyled text rather than as restraint.

    Listing both here is what keeps them one design. Copying the block and
    keeping the two in step by hand is how `coverageTags` and
    `youtubeSyncKeywords` drifted, and a padding or border change here would
    silently apply to only one of the two buttons standing side by side.

    Recreated to match the featured page.
  */
  .feed-hero-youtube-btn,
  .feed-hero-watch-btn {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.45rem;
    /* Matches .feed-hero-youtube-glyph's height. SUBSCRIBE is as tall as its
       glyph, not as tall as its text, so without this the button beside it
       comes out 3px shorter: measured 30px against 27px at 1440x900. Set on
       the shared rule rather than on the glyphless one, so the two cannot be
       given different line boxes later. */
    line-height: 15px;
    margin-top: 0.15rem; /* Matches .hub-stage-replay */
    padding: 0.5rem 1.05rem 0.5rem 0.9rem;
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.28);
    border-radius: 0;
    background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.32);
    color: var(--color-white, #f0f0f0);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: 0.62rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    text-decoration: none;
    cursor: pointer;
    transition: border-color 0.25s ease, background 0.25s ease;
  }

  .feed-hero-youtube-btn:hover,
  .feed-hero-youtube-btn:focus-visible,
  .feed-hero-watch-btn:hover,
  .feed-hero-watch-btn:focus-visible {
    border-color: rgba(var(--brand-rgb, 204, 0, 0), 0.85);
    background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5);
  }

  .feed-hero-youtube-glyph {
    width: 15px;
    height: 15px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }

  .feed-hero-youtube-btn:focus-visible,
  .feed-hero-watch-btn:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--color-accent-text, #ef4444);
    outline-offset: 3px;
  }

  /*
    The one declaration the two do NOT share. SUBSCRIBE is padded tighter on
    its left for the glyph that sits there; this button has no glyph, so the
    same asymmetry would read as a box with its label pushed off centre.
  */
  .feed-hero-watch-btn {
    padding-left: 1.05rem;
  }

  .hub-stage-mark-img {
    width: 62%;
    max-height: 78%;
    height: auto;
    object-fit: contain;
  }

  /*
    ─── ART MODE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    The same layer, holding the key art instead of a mark.

    It CLIPS, and that is safe HERE and only here: `.hub-stage-mark` is a
    SIBLING of `.hub-stage-video`, never its ancestor, so hard rule 3 is not
    in play. The plate inside overscans past the clip because a CSS blur goes
    weak at its own edges — the outermost band is the least blurred part — and
    left flush, that near-sharp band is exactly the edge of the frame, where
    it would read as a sharp picture with a soft middle.

    `filter: none` because the base rule's drop-shadow glow is shaped for a
    mark with transparent surroundings. On a full-bleed rectangle it is just a
    shadow, and `box-shadow` draws the same thing without a filter's repaint
    cost. It is the video's own shadow, so idle and playing sit in one frame.
  */
  .hub-stage-mark--art {
    /*
      NO FILTER. The base rule's drop-shadow glow is shaped for a mark
      floating in transparency; on a full-bleed rectangle it is just a
      shadow, which `box-shadow` draws without a filter's repaint cost. That
      shadow is the video's own, so the idle frame and the playing one are
      the same object rather than two boxes that share a position.
    */
    filter: none;
    -webkit-filter: none;
    opacity: 1;
    box-shadow: 0 30px 70px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.85);
    /*
      Clips the blurred fill behind the art. Safe HERE and only here:
      `.hub-stage-mark` is a SIBLING of `.hub-stage-video`, never its
      ancestor, so hard rule 3 is not in play. The stage itself still never
      clips.
    */
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  /*
    ─── THE PLACEHOLDER IS THE PICTURE, AND ALL OF IT ──────────────────────

    It shipped blurred and overscanned, borrowing the treatment every other
    plate on this page uses. That was wrong twice: it was an effect applied to
    the event's art rather than the art, and the overscan zoomed it. Asked for
    plainly: "it should just be the full image that is used for the hero but
    in the location of the trailer at the trailer's size."

    Then it was `cover`, and that cut L.A. Comic Con's key art in half. The
    frame is 16/9 and 17 of the 19 events' art is exactly 16:9, so cover was
    an exact fit almost everywhere and the exceptions were the whole problem:
    L.A. Comic Con is 2.35:1 and SXSW is 2.70:1, and cover trimmed 24% and
    34% off their sides. Both have their NAME set across the full width of
    the artwork, so what cover removed was the first and last letters of the
    event's own title. Reported, correctly, as "cut off".

    So the front copy CONTAINS: the whole image, never cropped, whatever
    shape it is. `contain` alone would leave bars, which is why there are two
    copies — see the fill below. On the 17 events whose art is already 16:9,
    contain and cover are the same fit and the fill is never visible at all.
  */
  .hub-stage-art-img {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: contain;
    object-position: center;
    display: block;
  }

  /*
    THE GUTTERS ARE FILLED, NOT LEFT EMPTY.

    Letterbox bars in the frame a trailer is about to play in read as a
    broken image, and a flat black bar reads as a rendering fault. A blurred,
    darkened copy of the same file behind the art is the standard answer, and
    it costs no extra request: identical src and srcset, so the browser
    paints one decode twice.

    It is DARKENED as well as blurred, deliberately. The gutter has to recede
    or it competes with the picture it is framing, and this page has a
    recorded failure in the other direction: the hero was once a black
    rectangle because a dark image was blurred and then dimmed. That applied
    to the thing carrying the scene. This is the margin around it.

    `scale(1.12)` on the IMAGE, never on the clipping box. A CSS blur samples
    the transparent pixels outside its element and goes weak at its own
    edges, so the fill is pushed past the clip where that band is never seen.
    Scaling the clip instead is what caused the light leak on the deck page.
  */
  .hub-stage-art-fill {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: center;
    display: block;
    transform: scale(1.12);
    filter: blur(22px) saturate(1.1) brightness(0.62);
    -webkit-filter: blur(22px) saturate(1.1) brightness(0.62);
  }

  .hub-stage-wordmark {
    font-family: var(--font-display, sans-serif);
    font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 3.6vw, 3rem);
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    text-align: center;
    color: rgba(255,255,255,0.92);
    line-height: 1.15;
  }

  .hub-stage-video {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    /* Was an inline `style="z-index: 5"` on the element. Moved here because an
       inline declaration outranks every stylesheet rule, so the PiP rule's
       z-index could never take effect and the floating window sat under the
       page furniture. */
    z-index: 5;
    opacity: 0;
    transform: scale(1.02);
    transition: opacity 1.1s ease, transform 1.1s cubic-bezier(0.2, 0.8, 0.2, 1);
    box-shadow: 0 30px 70px rgba(0,0,0,0.85);
    /* NO overflow: hidden and no radius needing one. HARD RULE 3. */
  }

  /* ─── PICTURE IN PICTURE ────────────────────────────────────────────────

     The stage's video layer detaches to the bottom-right of the VIEWPORT while
     a video is playing and the hero has scrolled away.

     The iframe is never moved in the DOM and never re-sourced — doing either
     restarts playback, which is the whole thing this feature exists to avoid.
     Only this wrapper's positioning changes, so the same element keeps playing
     the same frame throughout.

     `position: fixed` here relies on no ancestor of .hub-stage-video
     establishing a containing block (a transform, filter, perspective,
     backdrop-filter or will-change on an ancestor would trap it into the hero
     instead of the viewport). `.hub-stage-mark` carries transforms, but it is a
     SIBLING of this element, not an ancestor.

     And no `overflow: hidden` anywhere in this rule set: hard rule 3 says a
     clipping ancestor turns a cross-origin YouTube iframe into a black box on
     iOS Safari, and the rounded corner that would normally want clipping is
     therefore drawn as a border instead. */
  /*
    THE ESCAPE HATCH FOR `position: fixed`.

    `.event-hero-overlay` carries `backdrop-filter: blur(3px)`, and any non-none
    backdrop-filter makes an element a containing block for fixed-position
    DESCENDANTS. The stage lives inside it, so the PiP window anchored itself to
    the hero rather than the viewport: measured at 1440x900 it sat 338px above
    the bottom edge instead of 28px, and it would have scrolled away with the
    hero it was supposed to outlive.

    The blur is dropped only while a window is out, and it is set from JS rather
    than through `:has()` on purpose. `:has()` would read better, but where it
    is unsupported the rule silently does nothing and the window is positioned
    off screen. A class toggled by the same function that toggles `is-pip`
    cannot get out of step with it and works everywhere.

    Nothing is lost visually: the hero is by definition scrolled out of view
    whenever this applies.
  */
  .event-hero-overlay.is-pip-host {
    backdrop-filter: none;
    -webkit-backdrop-filter: none;
  }

  .hub-stage.is-pip .hub-stage-video {
    position: fixed;
    top: auto;
    left: auto;
    right: clamp(12px, 3vw, 28px);
    /*
      ─── IT STOPS ON THE FOOTER, IT DOES NOT CROSS IT ───────────────────────

      `--pip-lift` is how far the footer has risen into the viewport, written by
      the scroll handler below. At rest it is 0 and this is the plain inset the
      window has always had; as the footer arrives the window rides up ahead of
      it and comes to a stop on its top edge.

      Lifting is the fix rather than a z-index. The window was rendering BEHIND
      the footer, and raising it above would have left it sitting ON the footer,
      over the site's own links. Neither is right: it should never share pixels
      with the footer in either direction.
    */
    bottom: calc(clamp(12px, 3vw, 28px) + var(--pip-lift, 0px));
    /*
      ─── BIG ENOUGH FOR YOUTUBE'S OWN CHROME ────────────────────────────────

      This was `min(360px, 42vw)`, which is 164x92 on a 390px phone. YouTube's
      embedded player stops laying out properly somewhere around 200x113: below
      that its overlay (title, channel, CC, pause, progress, logo, captions)
      cannot fit the box, and the player rescales the video to make room for
      it. Reported as the video being cropped WHILE the overlay is visible and
      correcting itself when it fades -- which is that rescale arriving and
      leaving, not our box changing shape.

      The floor only binds on phones. Anything above about 524px wide still
      gets 42vw, and the 360px ceiling is unchanged.
    */
    width: min(360px, max(220px, 42vw));
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
    height: auto;
    inset-inline-start: auto;
    z-index: 90;
    /* Beats .hub-stage-video's own z-index: 5 above by specificity, not by
       !important. */
    opacity: 1;
    transform: none;
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.18);
    box-shadow: 0 24px 60px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.75);
    transition: opacity 0.3s ease;
  }

  /* Below the phone breakpoint a 42vw window is too small to read and too easy
     to fat-finger, so it spans the gutter width instead. */
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .hub-stage.is-pip .hub-stage-video {
      width: min(240px, 62vw);
    }
  }

  .hub-stage-pip-close {
    position: absolute;
    top: -12px;
    right: -12px;
    z-index: 2;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    width: 26px;
    height: 26px;
    padding: 0;
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.28);
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: #0a0a0a;
    color: var(--color-white, #f0f0f0);
    cursor: pointer;
    transition: border-color 0.25s ease, background 0.25s ease;
  }

  .hub-stage-pip-close:hover,
  .hub-stage-pip-close:focus-visible {
    border-color: rgba(var(--brand-rgb, 204, 0, 0), 0.9);
    background: #141414;
  }

  .hub-stage-pip-close:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--color-accent-text, #ef4444);
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .hub-stage.is-pip .hub-stage-video,
    .hub-stage-pip-close {
      transition: none;
    }
  }

  .hub-stage-iframe {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    border: 0;
    display: block;
    /* A frame whose document never loads paints WHITE by default, which is a
       white rectangle in the middle of a black page. */
    background: #050505;
    /*
      ─── AND TELL SAFARI THE FRAME ITSELF IS DARK ─────────────────────────

      The background above paints the iframe ELEMENT. It does not reach the
      document inside it, and on iOS Safari a cross-origin frame renders its
      own `about:blank` on a white canvas until YouTube's CSS arrives, so the
      white flashed over the black we just set. `color-scheme` is the one
      property that crosses that boundary: it tells the embedded document
      which canvas colour to default to before it has said anything itself.
    */
    color-scheme: dark;
  }

  /* Playing: video up, mark down but not out — it is the glow behind it. */
  .hub-stage.is-playing .hub-stage-video {
    opacity: 1;
    transform: scale(1);
  }
  .hub-stage.is-playing .hub-stage-mark {
    opacity: 0.28;
    transform: scale(1.06);
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .hub-stage-mark,
    .hub-stage-video {
      transition: none;
    }
  }

  /*
    Below the 50/50 breakpoint the grid stacks, so the feathered plate has no
    right half to sit in. It becomes the full-bleed ground behind the stacked
    copy instead, which is what the deck page does at these sizes.

    This condition is the EXACT INVERSE of the split query above, and has to
    stay that way. It used to be a flat `max-width: 1023px`, which overlapped
    the split by a whole band: a 900-1023px portrait viewport got the two
    column layout AND the full-bleed plate, so the right-hand column sat on a
    backdrop built for a stacked page.
  */
  @media (max-width: 899px) and (orientation: portrait), (max-width: 819px) {
    .hub-stage-wash {
      background: radial-gradient(120% 70% at 50% 38%,
        rgba(var(--brand-rgb, 204,0,0), 0.16) 0%,
        rgba(var(--brand-rgb, 204,0,0), 0.05) 46%,
        transparent 74%);
    }
  }

  /*
    THE MARK IN THE COPY COLUMN.

    Sizing is left to the .hero-logo rule further down, which already scales
    with the viewport, caps its width so a wide mark cannot overflow the column,
    and carries a note about iOS. A second height here just fought it.

    HARD RULE 6: the glow is on the WRAPPER, never on the <img>. The rule below
    notes that a filter ON the image made it fail to render on iOS entirely.
  */
  .hero-logo-wrap {

    display: block;
    margin: 0.2rem 0 1.4rem;
    filter: drop-shadow(0 10px 26px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.9));
  }

  /*
    MARK ABOVE THE LINE, TIGHT TOGETHER.

    Side by side was tried and rejected: it squeezed the description into a
    narrow column, which made it wrap enough to trip the "Read more" clamp on
    hubs with longer copy — an expand control nobody wants in a hero. Full
    width beneath the mark, the line always fits and the clamp never fires.

    The gap is deliberately small. The two belong to each other; the air
    between them was reading as two separate things.
  */
  .hero-identity {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: 0.55rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.2rem;
  }
  .hero-identity .hero-logo-wrap {
    margin: 0;
  }

  /*
    A HEADING THAT IS PURELY A CONTAINER. The <h1> around the mark must not
    bring a heading's own margin, size or weight with it — the alt text only
    ever paints if the image fails, and it should land where the mark would
    have, not as a browser-default 2em block that moves everything under it.
  */
  .hero-title-lockup {
    display: block;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    font-size: inherit;
    font-weight: inherit;
    line-height: inherit;
    letter-spacing: inherit;
  }

  /* ─── THE RAIL ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .hub-rail {
    display: flex;
    gap: 10px;
    /*
      `min-width: 0` IS LOAD-BEARING.

      A grid `1fr` track is `minmax(auto, 1fr)`, so it cannot shrink below its
      content's min-content width. This rail is five 108px cards plus four 10px
      gaps = exactly 580px of min-content, which is precisely what the copy
      column measured while the stage next to it collapsed from 600px to 290px
      on an iPad. The rail was quietly setting the column widths.

      It already scrolls horizontally, so allowing it to shrink costs nothing —
      the same automatic-minimum trap that made the accordion rows size
      themselves off their sibling count on /featured.
    */
    min-width: 0;
    margin-top: 1.6rem;
    overflow-x: auto;
    /*
      PADDING ON ALL FOUR SIDES, AND EVERY SIDE IS LOAD-BEARING.

      `overflow-x: auto` computes `overflow-y` to `auto` as well, so this box
      clips vertically too — and the active card lifts AND scales 1.06. Without
      room, the selected tile was sliced: off the top always, and off the left
      or right when the selected card was the first or last one.

      The horizontal padding is what stops the end cards being cut. 1.06 on a
      108px card grows it ~3px each side, so 10px is comfortable margin.
    */
    padding: 10px;

    /*
      THE EDGE FEATHER ONLY EXISTS WHERE THERE IS MORE TO SCROLL TO.

      Both ramps default to 0%, so a rail that fits is not faded at all, and a
      rail scrolled hard left has no fade on its left. This is the same
      construct as the hub rail on /featured, and for the same reason: a fixed
      10%/90% ramp there dimmed the first and last item permanently, so two of
      five hubs looked disabled with nothing scrolled. Selecting an end card
      must never dim it.
    */
    -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(to right,
      transparent 0%, #000 var(--rail-fade-start, 0%),
      #000 calc(100% - var(--rail-fade-end, 0%)), transparent 100%);
    mask-image: linear-gradient(to right,
      transparent 0%, #000 var(--rail-fade-start, 0%),
      #000 calc(100% - var(--rail-fade-end, 0%)), transparent 100%);
    -webkit-mask-repeat: no-repeat;
    mask-repeat: no-repeat;
    scrollbar-width: none;
    -ms-overflow-style: none;
  }
  .hub-rail::-webkit-scrollbar { display: none; }

  .hub-rail-card {
    position: relative;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 108px;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
    padding: 0;
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.14);
    background: #111;
    cursor: pointer;
    opacity: 0.55;
    transition: opacity 0.3s ease, border-color 0.3s ease, transform 0.3s ease;
  }
  .hub-rail-card:hover,
  .hub-rail-card:focus-visible { opacity: 0.85; }
  /*
    Matches the deck rail on /featured: lift, brand border, brand glow — plus a
    caret above the selected tile so the link between the rail and the stage is
    stated rather than implied.
  */
  .hub-rail-card.active {
    opacity: 1;
    border-color: rgba(var(--brand-rgb, 204,0,0), 0.95);
    transform: scale(1.06);
    box-shadow: 0 5px 15px rgba(var(--brand-rgb, 204,0,0), 0.42);
  }
  .hub-rail-card.active::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: 50%;
    top: -9px;
    width: 0;
    height: 0;
    transform: translateX(-50%);
    border-left: 6px solid transparent;
    border-right: 6px solid transparent;
    border-top: 6px solid rgba(var(--brand-rgb, 204,0,0), 0.95);
    pointer-events: none;
  }
  .hub-rail-thumb {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    display: block;
  }
  .hub-rail-thumb--empty {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    background: linear-gradient(150deg, rgba(var(--brand-rgb, 204,0,0), 0.30) 0%, #0b0b0d 70%);
  }
  .hub-rail-label {
    position: absolute;
    left: 5px;
    bottom: 4px;
    font-size: 0.52rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.16em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: rgba(255,255,255,0.9);
    text-shadow: 0 1px 4px rgba(0,0,0,0.95);
  }

  /* ─── THE PANES THE RAIL SWITCHES TO ────────────────────────────────── */
  .hub-stage-item {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    opacity: 0;
    visibility: hidden;
    transition: opacity 0.7s ease;
    /* NO overflow: hidden — HARD RULE 3, the iframe is a sibling in here. */
  }
  .hub-stage-item.active {
    opacity: 1;
    visibility: visible;
    /* The whole pane runs its Play/Read control (src/lib/stage-item-tap.ts). */
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .hub-stage-item-still {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
  }
  .hub-stage-item-scrim {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background: linear-gradient(to top,
      rgba(5,5,5,0.94) 0%, rgba(5,5,5,0.86) 14%, rgba(5,5,5,0.66) 30%,
      rgba(5,5,5,0.40) 46%, rgba(5,5,5,0.18) 62%, rgba(5,5,5,0.05) 78%,
      transparent 92%);
  }
  .hub-stage-item-copy {
    position: absolute;
    left: 1.4rem;
    right: 1.4rem;
    bottom: 1.2rem;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: 0.55rem;
  }
  .hub-stage-item-kind {
    font-size: 0.6rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.22em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: rgba(var(--brand-rgb, 204,0,0), 1);
  }
  .hub-stage-item-title {
    font-family: var(--font-display, sans-serif);
    font-size: clamp(0.95rem, 1.5vw, 1.25rem);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.3;
    color: #fff;
    text-shadow: 0 2px 10px rgba(0,0,0,0.95);
    display: -webkit-box;
    -webkit-line-clamp: 2;
    line-clamp: 2;
    -webkit-box-orient: vertical;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .hub-stage-item-action {
    display: inline-block;
