    They were a blunt instrument aimed at the overflow fixed above: clamping
    hid the symptom by cutting the text off before it could reach the edge, so
    the real cause (a 510px measure inside a 335px column) stayed. With the
    measure fixed the text wraps properly, and the clamp can go back to doing
    its actual job — bounding the hero's height so a long title cannot push the
    stage off the screen — rather than truncating everything on sight.

    The two get DIFFERENT bounds, because they are different kinds of text.

    The TITLE is a headline and wants to be read in full: four lines fits this
    one ("Lanterns Episode 5 Review | Hal Jordan's Fate and the Future of the
    DCU") completely at 375px, where three cut it off mid-word at "the Future
    O…". It stays clamped rather than unbounded so a freak 20-word title still
    cannot shove the stage off a phone screen.

    The DESCRIPTION is a teaser and CANNOT be shown in full here: for a video
    it is the entire YouTube description, timestamps, gear list, affiliate
    links and all, which measures over 3000px tall on a phone. Three lines is
    the deliberate trim, and the point of the fix above is that those three
    lines now wrap inside the column instead of running off the screen.
  */
  .hero-title,
  .hero-tagline {
    display: -webkit-box;
    -webkit-box-orient: vertical;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .hero-title {
    -webkit-line-clamp: 4;
    line-clamp: 4;
  }

  .hero-tagline {
    -webkit-line-clamp: 3;
    line-clamp: 3;
  }

  .hero-tagline {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.22em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.58);
    line-height: 1.7;
    /*
      ─── `min(…, 100%)`, NOT A BARE `52ch` ─────────────────────────────────

      52ch is the reading measure and it is worth keeping, but a bare max-width
      in `ch` does not know how wide the column actually is. `.hero-identity`
      above is a COLUMN flex container with `align-items: flex-start`, which
      means its children are sized by their own content rather than stretched
      to the container. So this line sized itself to the only limit it had —
      52ch, which at this font size and 0.22em of letter-spacing resolves to
      510px — inside a column that is 335px wide on a 375px phone.

      The result was the description running off the right edge of the screen,
      mid-word, with no way to read the rest: the element clips
      (`overflow: hidden` for the line clamp), so it did not even extend the
      page to reveal itself. The document never scrolled horizontally, which is
      exactly why this survived so long. It does not LOOK like an overflow bug,
      it looks like the copy was written too long.

      `min(52ch, 100%)` keeps the measure wherever there is room for it and
      hands the column back control everywhere else. The title next to it was
      already fine; this was the only element in the hero clearing the viewport.
    */
    max-width: min(52ch, 100%);
    margin-top: 1.6rem;
    text-shadow: 0 4px 12px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.9);
  }


